#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0187a97f-58b6-5b57-aaf9-ef89c90383f8" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "63ff719f-1f54-5370-854e-480379c93e96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5802" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b6886faa1ecd42e9539b9f6867ff0f629f6ae99366d8b9f397f82c5212ebffd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ca7245-a828-5bbf-9a6a-dcd1938333a2" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "1877c35b-7b3a-55dd-a81e-2b98cf3572bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5805" :question/problem "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=x^3+x^2-8x-12,$ graph the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x).$ To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & -8 & -12 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 12 \\\\  & 1 & 4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-3} & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)(x+2)^2.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/bd/bd9f011708eb389611f4c12df70ddfd318039b58b5cbfd7fcb3980a906b0ad75.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0c71e95-0a4a-547f-aaf0-774c6fe694a4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "0ef12435-c090-5766-a58a-f913d6e02421" :question/math-academy-id "e-5807" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(3-x)(x+1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(3-0)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*. If we were to expand the parentheses, we'd get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(3-x)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/14/14c1e6701c5f52f37788d22a5e09ba0bc92897ea64fa64ce0f3717115d9604af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c85a009-fe0d-5c56-ac1d-a2a00002f94e" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "35d9d449-6508-5461-89bb-d56c262e6bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5809" :question/problem "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-x^3 + 2x^2 + 7x + 4,$ graph the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x).$ To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & 7 & 4 \\\\  &  & -4 & -8 & -4 \\\\  & -1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\ \\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\ f(x) & =-(x-4)({x}^{2}+2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x) = - (x-4)(x+1)^2.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=-1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-4)(0+1)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-4)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29091d81f99dc2b99787194bb158cc9a2f850241c5b50ae9ad5ec3f94f11603f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0187a97f-58b6-5b57-aaf9-ef89c90383f8" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06413641-cb6c-59bd-b7c7-a2ec01cc1f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/ebb182491e9eebf8509d3354d31ec6cda4af9921cc3e1c527ac4fe8c1e7f9bd0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556444} {:answer/id #uuid "7469e963-5028-57a9-906d-251e526d81f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48cb7502e7985edd92acf72c52ebc056c2dfd823118fa2bbf910a6e7903597a7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556693} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a591d1-98a7-5248-9112-9e31ca903999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21d44b71cc74249d45910247627ed30f0f090364b209ba56470546af39619c29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556897} {:answer/id #uuid "d42b25d8-e67e-5a18-b32d-18507555153a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556943} {:answer/id #uuid "eae50afa-0ea3-5f8a-a64a-50bd9c93e83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5f87eac1680cf001b5cfe649d9aa18b713b69eade8d626b108d968b2d220ca3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d42b25d8-e67e-5a18-b32d-18507555153a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556943} :answer-field/id #uuid "561d7a9f-05aa-5181-8cd5-fc83e0f35882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ec517e9-4418-5776-9805-f9f4171a3217" :question/math-academy-id "q-112131" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-3)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/baff75d3cb8119a39819b82cca053f686ce33ebfc86f963422e1a0653a9f842b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ca7245-a828-5bbf-9a6a-dcd1938333a2" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3a6199-7669-5754-afee-1653731f9e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e8cc20b39330100b9ce1b2e258f41527f8d7ebbaa4050f87838d68c98b41e53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556461} {:answer/id #uuid "11730dda-46b1-59fe-b211-2faca9c98f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/8835508bde7ea7167978fa3d17243daeef591fd36c4ed72538420127b3f7750d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556464} {:answer/id #uuid "3287860e-2c1a-5b77-9ed9-665cdd9f41c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556536} {:answer/id #uuid "af81d9b5-26a0-5bd7-aa2b-1fb2d737fc1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf493c2853bf7d4e229f2b66430c5a676478ff5fb158b35685b32d266257f883.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556861} {:answer/id #uuid "dd539f80-f263-5f13-b6da-89c7d1498a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a569d7c9c245db3c5c06772b703956c1d021f8d7d1c08bea071cc071499b812.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3287860e-2c1a-5b77-9ed9-665cdd9f41c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff339910-4fbb-5844-b100-4c1b4c7881f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6ba8710-6e4b-5d1b-8f82-9bf7f2de8b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112137" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x-4,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -6 & 9 & -4 \\\\  &  & 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\  & 1 & -5 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}-5x+4\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-5x+4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(x-1)(x-4)=(x-4)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=4$ and a double root at $x=1.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)(0-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(-4)(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62b826632a520d1c1d2180b62e528b2a9d5264d6102419822a5194e90196d9f2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ca7245-a828-5bbf-9a6a-dcd1938333a2" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ae4acd-da40-5a3a-a650-919674ee0667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c205eb2555ee72e52142aa0f97037b8ca1690d188e7fadb7e384c949817d037.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556427} {:answer/id #uuid "cc094071-c587-5788-abd9-0c387eaf9306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2e5b06b196bba9019b3ea2f33e372ca05cb791bd09f42e32954efe97fda05135.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556921} {:answer/id #uuid "e81ef1d7-aa58-5637-913c-7c96c2f820d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7dd3a15f3dc313e1234e388432c974dbc2052878e67783f2afea5ac570a29f53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557003} {:answer/id #uuid "f54318ee-4bea-54a0-b85c-ec775d748a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/7384284052f745b189b7a127337cd133397e48dca2a2c8c7908c8ab36de46b19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557035} {:answer/id #uuid "ff934f05-ee80-50d2-b0d2-9adb79642869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff934f05-ee80-50d2-b0d2-9adb79642869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557062} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f99a7f-d42d-56d7-a9f1-5ae9c59b98af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3aad7acd-4801-580e-a95b-e6983a14c705" :question/math-academy-id "q-112145" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 18 \\\\  &  & -2 & 12 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & -6 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+2}={x}^{2}-6x+9\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)({x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(x-3)(x-3)=(x+2)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-2$ and a double root at $x=3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+2)(0-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,18),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/266609da7c307b98b8d5f280c75f6d8fd557c23b2e2e1885be37fa4ee3d7b229.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c85a009-fe0d-5c56-ac1d-a2a00002f94e" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121516ae-44e0-52e3-948a-fbb82bd8420e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48e4fc68dcd32d250158e27f873af285bc88fa30ff959f164c2db30d7c63e9e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556466} {:answer/id #uuid "235ba100-d4c1-5618-8234-09762289b2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/16cb1fd207d56c24e409af4a559b6401627574772c27078d808a08459aa57f37.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556510} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9a3f71-fa92-5198-a731-22611ab2f298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b18e2b945ba8555b4a6de2d8fed5a394fa3bfb8af299c0a22c15fe4ecda2564d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556566} {:answer/id #uuid "61fb7bab-ed18-5a05-b18c-b8c4bafb68c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556654} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c5a969-e226-575d-8194-95b240094b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51d68b8563124c14cdd03a299fcd402225fee11e91094db1d889157e13db9817.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61fb7bab-ed18-5a05-b18c-b8c4bafb68c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556654} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2e86d8-7763-51a4-ae33-886c3ae607fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb4e0d8f-3854-5a60-be7f-5cac9da0624a" :question/math-academy-id "q-112166" :question/problem "Given that $x+2$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+8x+12,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & -1 & 8 & 12 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & -12 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+2} & =-{x}^{2}+x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+2)(x+2)(x-3)=-(x-3)(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=3$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-3)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-3)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/99/99063a71f1a368c2f27bd9b4d3817ea8f39e34789d26074f70c68333667cd90f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0c71e95-0a4a-547f-aaf0-774c6fe694a4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36e29128-9612-5621-b4dd-57ec7ce0df13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/37a1389b38a3961bfd810ae221f4ed20aa8ee6186f2dbf8ec6ce9543ab90c3d3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556549} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcb05d7-50cf-50a3-bc08-d6e5f0e459e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d2d8ced0961eb8c526b2cc2b82518fe039c67fae63ca0b76d8aaaa9bca1e1b4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556561} {:answer/id #uuid "500b984a-74c4-53b0-99f6-7ba49df010c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556610} {:answer/id #uuid "6fea984a-57b4-5fc2-8d51-df4673eb7fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a87746ce937bdf3e9b4153b3114d360fa6ca0358ac5ac648d7c43a77f87f6c5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556684} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c572a9-faaf-5aa7-9320-38b9b98cef3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e1789d9797aab42d961e451ba33fd1e65f41e4b0fbebe86f51968a0460018a1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "500b984a-74c4-53b0-99f6-7ba49df010c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556610} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee28e45-0132-5eed-a906-a92a5d72c6e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de17e3d5-60e1-5541-9c18-3de6f1f6965c" :question/math-academy-id "q-27994" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(2-x)(1-x)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(2-0)(1-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=1,$ and\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-x)(1-x)^{2} \\\\  & =(2-x)({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/78/78af8854b6dd0da9eeded23cbcfa223018ca425d13d802b9e72039d906976bde.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0187a97f-58b6-5b57-aaf9-ef89c90383f8" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8266b81-894e-5d79-94d5-7704a51f679c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df39777d556b266991428a735eeacf46304ce51b2526f402d93e208288cb6a66.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113137} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cabc78-b08b-5809-bdb4-c69345cb9c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/021fddfcb24a74f7ed6ce4007561844375089b81c3aa1d6a42b6da9dc769df90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113138} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c36563-89e6-586f-ae83-004010e49495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1ae0c05746c113c2978afb5c4163dd8e460e36dabd0fbed15bb86b9d9f3aa34b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113139} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa89160-d114-5d7c-91b8-d05050d88ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0d67532b836411bab32b7c2039c4cce75aa22ba1f54ddbc6661b811f023c95f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113140} {:answer/id #uuid "3292067f-f00b-5c28-aec4-62067be83ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/7907855732f87ccac6febf7b90c64b91b4ba5c5ca5b26e3496162839c2d706ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cabc78-b08b-5809-bdb4-c69345cb9c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/021fddfcb24a74f7ed6ce4007561844375089b81c3aa1d6a42b6da9dc769df90.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113138} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c202c0-13aa-577a-bcd3-36c65a6500c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d74891d1-4f12-551a-bae1-2f54778f9862" :question/math-academy-id "q-28529" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(x+4)(x+2)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=-4$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(0+4)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4)(4) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/02/021fddfcb24a74f7ed6ce4007561844375089b81c3aa1d6a42b6da9dc769df90.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0c71e95-0a4a-547f-aaf0-774c6fe694a4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0562bacd-93f9-54b9-b7b8-73da02fa84b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3ec4938c8981dce125d97c031240e3a4b4ee6cf2ca7e60c7ca7717b44bff7511.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556441} {:answer/id #uuid "a78a6516-11f2-5751-b18e-f936666d6657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/440f59b2b5ec62f140b60f91e96d39d85bf4b708972ffaa7a1173da46065598e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556845} {:answer/id #uuid "c26a74fb-36d6-5500-8dd2-c54665870413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/626880abe7c3c6c89453d63701193715e7f5ad437e75ffb4d821caad072d96d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556899} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc9f773-686d-5982-9e21-2cb304f5b3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556930} {:answer/id #uuid "d72140f3-dba4-5ef4-a028-fda2b3798dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ecd8c42e621eb97647249c42cc172c74f3adedbc4a6312ece1e0123013572ab3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfc9f773-686d-5982-9e21-2cb304f5b3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556930} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3ed2cb-8888-5412-974a-3f9e4de7b1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff659825-07ff-583c-bc20-8ee310e82141" :question/math-academy-id "q-75155" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)(3+0)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nNotice that the leading coefficient is *negative*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)(3+x)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+6x+9) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dc748825dca638e83ad9e09d7144365abbb3024ccb6474673c0557e3e1c21325.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c85a009-fe0d-5c56-ac1d-a2a00002f94e" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d476839-88f3-5d95-af60-1c6347ff4ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1bf83d0704c39f940bef9d32518fb89903ec5e68040a8d60df6c8a18194cde6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556527} {:answer/id #uuid "64e81793-590f-5b29-ac89-461f32a3e98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06b7c566fce9906b86314ed7e2e8655e63c16a67247041dd658b209f93e699a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556662} {:answer/id #uuid "91d89bba-6aea-5df5-bb7c-dac9538b260b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4441651a590fc45f3d42832fb9247d6932fe2999a3c4310d504c7bb53ee0e3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556780} {:answer/id #uuid "d6be3318-096c-5fdd-a537-f4c24057a2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88f17b45290320d63748005ee10d60441a2eee5f4936d286cb984aa890271bde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556956} {:answer/id #uuid "fcea6cc2-ba7c-528e-b045-a8d2f1343f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/898496eef07ddef338c737819dba7d34db4fe58f8860c62902204ee1d70583f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6be3318-096c-5fdd-a537-f4c24057a2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88f17b45290320d63748005ee10d60441a2eee5f4936d286cb984aa890271bde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556956} :answer-field/id #uuid "7abee97d-691d-584c-b315-998bdc941618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cca0427-a573-5822-8e99-bd72fe2c51d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75165" :question/problem "Given that $x-4$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-32x+32$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n*(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 10 & -32 & 32 \\\\  &  & -4 & 24 & -32 \\\\  & -1 & 6 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-6x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)({x}^{2}-6x+8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x-4)(x-2)(x-4)=-(x-2)(x-4)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=2$ and a double root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-(-2)(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,32),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aae66bc5dcf29e5e85e33942541b510d136b4f7f13d633f28c46d4b5a9ca977b.png)"}]}
